WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:12.740
Um, I was, uh, educated as a chemist.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:18.439
I did my undergraduate degree in, uh, chemistry
in the University of Science and Technology

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:19.439
of China.

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:29.800
And, around in, uh, 1998, I, uh, went to Howard
to, uh, uh, conduct, uh, research in the chemistry

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.660
department to get my PhD degree.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:40.600
And, uh, 2002, I moved to uni- University
of California at Berkeley, uh, to do my Post

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:47.899
Doc, uh, working on, uh, you know, nanoparticles,
uh, um, materials, electron transport.

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:53.260
2005, I, I joined the Stanford faculty in
Material Science department.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.859
That was the time, in 2005, I started to get
interested in the battery research.

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:08.420
Um, getting into the bat- battery areas in
around 2005 also, that was, uh, around the

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.500
time, uh, the, the, uh, uh, renewable energy
become so important.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.710
Clean energy is important.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.659
You know, solar cell research are, are very
high.

00:01:17.659 --> 00:01:22.140
So at that time, I, I was already thinking
about, you know, what’s, what’s next.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.190
And after you get the clean energy, you need
to store them.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:31.460
And, uh, having a car burning gasoline, you,
you want to have a clean energy such as battery

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.119
source to, uh, to power the car.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:39.310
That’s how I get into the battery area in
2005.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:51.759
Um, my group study very broad topic of research,
including batteries, solar cells, and environmental

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:56.579
technology, and, and the biotechnology, and
also quantum physics.

00:01:56.579 --> 00:01:59.759
Uh, I’m really passionate about batteries.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:07.620
Uh, uh, eh, it, it’s really because it’s
potential, and, uh, uh, and for the society.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:15.500
I, uh, currently have, uh, two category of
research going in the batteries, uh, areas.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:23.790
One side is to, uh, produce high energy batteries
for the portable electronics and the, uh,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.960
electrical vehicles through, uh, neo materials
design.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:33.260
Uh, those, uh, materials can have very high
capacity for storing lithium ions for example.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:41.069
The second category of research is for finding
neo materials very low cost, very high power,

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:51.099
uh, very high energy efficiency, for the,
uh, stationary, larger scale, uh, uh, storage.

00:02:51.099 --> 00:03:01.970
So, um, for, uh, students or for people, you
know, working industry, if they are interesting

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:08.099
in, uh, in the batteries, interesting in material
science part of that, certainly taking this,

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:12.470
uh, battery module will be the, uh, first
right thing to do.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:19.140
And, uh, reading some of the, uh, uh, uh,
basics about electrochemistry, about the batteries

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.950
is, uh, is necessary, you know, to get start.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.010
And then reading about the state of our research.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:29.260
You know, reading about the past development
of lead-acid batteries, lithium-ion batteries

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:36.980
are, uh, also, uh, uh, absolutely necessary
to, uh, to, uh, get to know what’s happening

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:46.489
in the field.

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:52.980
To develop a source of energy to power everything
in, in my life, that got to be electricity.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:59.590
Uh, yeah, electricity, you can produce them
clean, you can store them clean, you can use

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:00.590
them clean.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:01.590
That’s a fun question.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:02.590
So, how do we survive with absolutely no energy
use?

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:03.590
I think the best way is go to sleep!

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:03.900
[Laughs] Don’t consume energy.

